.Sh DESCRIPTION
An audit token,
.Vt audit_token_t
of
.In bsm/audit.h ,
is the identity of one process at one point of its life, kept by the
kernel's audit subsystem.
It is opaque: its layout is private to the kernel and to these functions,
and may change with them.
Tokens are handed out by
.Xr audit_token_get 2 ,
checked by
.Xr audit_token_check 2 ,
turned into a process descriptor by
.Xr pdopentoken 2 ,
and carried in the procident token of kernel audit records
.Pq see Xr audit.log 5 .
.Pp
A token holds three identifiers beside the process ID:
.Bl -tag -width "imageid"
.It uniqueid
the process's lifetime ID: set when it is created by
.Xr fork 2 ,
never changed, never given to another process.
.It execgen
the exec generation: new when the process is created and at every
successful
.Xr execve 2 .
.It imageid
the image ID of the process's credential: new at every successful
.Xr execve 2 ,
shared with the processes forked since (which inherit the credential), and
kept across
.Xr setuid 2
and the other credential changes that do not exec.
It is 0 for credentials the kernel makes, such as those of kernel
processes, which have no image.
.El
.Pp
All three are counters: within a boot each only grows, so a later value
means a later fork or exec.
Each boot starts them at a random offset (bits 62 to 32 random, the low 32
bits 0, bit 63 clear), so values are not comparable across boots, and a
token kept from an earlier boot matches a process of this one only by a
1 in 2^31 chance of the same offset.
The first process, the kernel's, has the first uniqueid and exec generation
of the boot.
They are not secrets: what a token allows is decided by whoever was handed
it, never by its being hard to guess.
A token names a process when its process ID, lifetime ID and exec generation
match
.Pq see Xr audit_token_check 2 .
.Pp
It also holds what the process had when the token was taken: the jail ID
.Pq 0 outside a jail ,
the effective and real user and group IDs, and the audit user and session
IDs.
.Pp
Each
.Fn audit_token_to_*
function returns the field its name gives.
.Fn audit_token_to_pidversion
is the macOS name for the exec generation and returns its low 32 bits;
.Fn audit_token_to_execgen
returns it whole.
.Pp
.Fn audit_token_to_au32
stores the fields the 32-bit BSM subject token holds through each pointer
that is not
.Dv NULL ;
a token has no terminal, so
.Fa tidp
gets this host and port 0.
